\title{Four squares through each edge}\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
We recover the prior bound $n(4,4,4)\ge21$ in Table~2, physical
page~18 of Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}. Here $n(k,4,\lambda)$
is the least order of a simple graph of degree $k$, girth four,
and constant square count $\lambda$ on its edges. The two acquired
completion methods~\cite{five,moments} are sufficient after a common
partition is extended from a five-cycle to an arbitrary shortest odd cycle.
The finite certificates below include every required order and alternative.

\begin{lemma}\label{partition}
Let $C=(v_0,\ldots,v_{\ell-1})$ be a shortest odd cycle in a
triangle-free graph, with $\ell\ge5$. Every vertex has at most two
neighbours on $C$. If it has two, they are separated by distance two
along $C$. In a graph of degree four on $n$ vertices, define $A_i$
by the neighbour pair $\{v_{i-1},v_{i+1}\}$, define $B_i$ by the
single neighbour $v_i$, and let $Z$ contain vertices with none.
Writing their sizes as $a_i,b_i,z$, one has
\[
 a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}+b_i=4,\quad
 \sum b_i+2z=2n-4\ell,\quad 1\le a_i\le3,\quad 0\le b_i\le2.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The bound of two cycle neighbours is the acquired shortest-odd-cycle
incidence lemma~\cite{cycles}. For an outside vertex with two neighbours,
one of the intervening arcs of $C$ has odd length. That arc together
with the two incident edges is an odd cycle, so the arc has length at
least $\ell-2$. The complementary even arc has positive length at
most two, hence exactly two. For a vertex of $C$, shortestness makes
$C$ induced, and its two neighbours already have the asserted form.
Thus the displayed classes partition the entire vertex set, and
$v_i\in A_i$.
The degree of $v_i$ gives the first equation. Summing it and using
$n=\sum a_i+\sum b_i+z$ gives the second. The remaining bounds follow
from the first and positivity of both adjacent $a$ entries.
\end{proof}

For each fixed odd $\ell$, the vector $b$ determines at most one
solution $a$. Indeed the difference of two solutions satisfies
$x_{i+2}=-x_i$, and hence has period four. Since four and $\ell$
are coprime, a cyclic solution is constant, and the original relation
then forces it to be zero. Consequently choosing the least dihedral
image of $b$ gives representatives of the full size patterns.
This reduces partitions only; it assumes no further automorphism of
a completed graph.

\begin{lemma}\label{finite}
No triangle-free graph of degree four and constant square count four
has order at most twenty and contains a shortest odd cycle.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The acquired incidence inequality $4\ell\le2n$ restricts the shortest
odd cycle to lengths five, seven or nine. The acquired edge-neighbourhood
bound gives $n\ge8+\lceil2(9-4)/4\rceil=11$.
For every indicated pair $(\ell,n)$, enumerate all
$a\in\{1,2,3\}^{\ell}$, compute $b_i=4-a_{i-1}-a_{i+1}$ and
$z=n-\sum a_i-\sum b_i$, and keep exactly the nonnegative solutions.
A separate enumeration chooses $z$ and the bounded weak compositions
of $2n-4\ell-2z$ for $b$, then solves the linear equations by trying
$a_0,a_1\in\{1,2,3\}$ and applying their recurrence. The two complete
labelled sets agree before the dihedral reduction.

Acquire the completion induction, active-cell symmetry, closed-edge
square intervals and convex moment test from~\cite{moments,five}.
Use the cycle vertices first, assign all remaining class labels and
insert precisely their prescribed cycle edges. Every cycle vertex is
closed. All undecided edges join vertices outside the cycle and can
be chosen by the same degree-completion induction. The initial pair
restriction forbids a common cycle neighbour, and the usual candidates
forbid present triangles. No additional missing edges are assumed.
In particular, the search is allowed to produce shorter odd cycles;
this only enlarges the class being excluded.

At each closed vertex, common-neighbour counts have total twelve
and factorial moment $4\lambda=16$. The acquired interval minimization
uses increment levels zero through three. The exact degree is four.
The longer-cycle implementation additionally replaces every cycle
length, cyclic index and initial active mask by its actual $\ell$.
Arrays and unsigned masks support all allowed orders. These are the
entire algorithmic changes, checked against the acquired source.
Thus all possible graphs satisfying Lemma~\ref{partition} survive
until either a proved necessary rejection or the exact final edge test.

The following totals include every size representative for every order
in the stated interval. All searches terminate, with zero retained
graphs and identical per-case records in ordinary and sanitized builds.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|r|r}
Cycle length&Orders&Size representatives&Search nodes\\\hline
5&11--20&104&\input{five_nodes.tex}\\
7&14--20&133&\input{seven_nodes.tex}\\
9&18--20&26&\input{nine_nodes.tex}
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The checker requires the complete ordered inventory and successful
termination of every case. The proof of coverage above, together with
these finite records, proves the exclusion.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{bipartite}
No bipartite graph of degree four on at most twenty vertices has
four squares through every edge.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let the two equal parts have size $m$. For a fixed vertex, common
neighbour counts with the other vertices of its part satisfy
$\sum c=12$ and $\sum c(c-1)=16$. If $m\le6$, pad to five
nonnegative counts. Convexity gives minimum factorial moment
$3(2\cdot1)+2(3\cdot2)=18$, a contradiction.
It remains to exclude $m=7,8,9,10$.

Use the complete incidence-row recursion proved in~\cite{five},
with row and column degree four. Equal current column profiles give
its permutation cells. For a selected edge $(i,j)$, its squares using
already selected rows number
\[
 L_{ij}=\sum_{\substack{h<r,\ h\ne i\\j\in R_h}}
                 (|R_i\cap R_h|-1).
\]
The additional squares are at most
$\sum_{t\in R_i\setminus\{j\}}\min(4-d_j,4-d_t)$,
where $d_j$ are the current column degrees. This is the same proved
incidence-capacity bound. At a complete matrix it becomes an exact
test. Every other step of the acquired induction and its implementation
is unchanged. The completed searches are
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{r|r|r}
Part size&Search nodes&Outputs\\\hline
7&8\,951&0\\8&174\,147&0\\9&2\,220\,392&0\\10&44\,551\,878&0
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
All per-case values agree in ordinary and sanitized builds. The first
short diagnostic at $m=10$ timed out; it is retained as such and is not
used as a certificate. The completed execution supplies the final row.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
\[n(4,4,4)\ge21.\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every graph is either bipartite or contains a shortest odd cycle.
Lemmas~\ref{finite} and~\ref{bipartite} exhaust these alternatives
below order twenty-one.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Verification and attribution.}
The numerical bound is prior Table~2 research in~\cite{GJ}.
The new work is the common shortest-odd-cycle partition and its finite
application, acquiring the complete earlier search proofs and their
implementation controls. No general priority claim is made.
Independent integer inventories and complete ordinary/sanitized executions
are included. Positive controls are the balanced size-two blowups of
cycles of lengths five, seven and nine (degree four and square count five),
$K_{4,4}$ (count nine), and the bipartite complement of a matching in
$K_{5,5}$ (count six). The written completeness proofs are essential;
positive examples alone establish no exclusion. This component adds one
lower-bound row, with no exact-count, whole-original, open-conjecture,
Lean or compression-measurement credit.
